{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The "Summarizing…" spinner that never once stopped spinning</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A meeting-notes tool adds a "Summarize with AI" button. Click it, a spinner appears with the label "Summarizing…", a few seconds later the summary replaces it. It works perfectly in every demo, every test account, every walkthrough anyone gave a customer.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then a product-wide email drives a wave of new usage at once, and the underlying model API starts returning <code>429 Too Many Requests</code>. The button's click handler <code>await</code>s the API call and, on success, replaces the spinner with the summary. Nobody wrote the failure branch. When the request fails, nothing runs, the spinner just keeps spinning, forever, because the code that would remove it only exists inside the success path.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      This is the most common shape a missing-fallback bug takes: not a crash, not a visible error, a UI stuck in a "loading" state that structurally cannot resolve because the code path that would resolve it never runs. Every user who clicked the button during the rate-limited window saw the exact same thing, at the exact same time, with no way to tell whether the page was broken or just slow.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The failure mode compounds because AI features usually call a single shared upstream, one rate limit or one outage doesn't degrade gracefully user-by-user, it fails simultaneously for everyone hitting that endpoint at that moment. A feature with no fallback path turns a transient, provider-side problem into a synchronized, product-wide outage the instant traffic crosses that provider's rate limit, exactly when traffic (and support ticket volume) is highest.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Handle the failure branch as a first-class UI state, not an afterthought</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      An AI call is a network request like any other, it can fail, and the UI needs a real plan for when it does: catch the error, stop showing "loading," and show something the user can act on, "Couldn't generate a summary right now, try again" with a working retry button, not a spinner with nowhere to go.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Treat the request as three distinct outcomes, not two: success, a clear failure the user can retry, and (where it's viable) a degraded but still-useful fallback that doesn't depend on the model at all, showing the raw notes unsummarized, or the last successfully generated summary from cache, so the feature keeps giving the user something rather than nothing.
    </p>
    <pre style="overflow-x:auto;background:var(--casebook-surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:8px;font-size:0.8125rem;"><code>async function summarize(notes) {
  setStatus('loading');
  try {
    const res = await fetch('/api/summarize', { body: notes, signal: AbortSignal.timeout(15000) });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`API returned ${res.status}`);
    setStatus('success');
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    setStatus('error');            // spinner clears no matter what happened
    return { fallback: notes };    // degraded path: raw notes, not nothing
  }
}</code></pre>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Distinguish error types where the response gives you one: a <code>429</code> with a <code>Retry-After</code> header is a temporary capacity problem worth an automatic backoff retry; a <code>4xx</code> from a malformed request is not something retrying will fix; a network timeout or <code>5xx</code> is provider-side and worth one or two retries with backoff before giving up. Collapsing all of these into one generic "error" state is fine for the user-facing copy, but the retry logic behind it should behave differently, or a transient rate limit ends up retried forever against a request that was never going to succeed, or a genuinely broken request gets retried needlessly against a provider that's already struggling.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>One rate limit, forty support tickets, zero code paths that could have shown an error</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The team's meeting-notes tool sent a feature-announcement email for "AI Summaries" to its full customer list on a Tuesday morning. Within twenty minutes, usage of the summarize button spiked far past anything the team's model-provider account had ever sustained, and the provider started returning <code>429</code> responses. The frontend's summarize handler had a success path and nothing else, no <code>catch</code>, no timeout, no fallback. Every rate-limited request left its button's spinner running indefinitely.
    </p>
    <p>
      Support started getting tickets within the hour, "the AI feature is broken," "stuck loading forever," "is the site down." Because the failure was silent (no error in the UI, no obvious crash), the on-call engineer's first reaction was to check whether the whole app was down, it wasn't, only this one feature, and only because of how it failed.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The rate limit itself cleared within about ninety minutes as the traffic spike settled, but the damage was in how many separate support conversations it generated for what was, underneath, a single well-understood, temporary condition. Nothing about the underlying failure was unusual for a provider API during a traffic spike, <code>429</code>s under load are expected and documented behavior, the bug was entirely that the frontend had never been written to expect one.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The fix added an explicit error state with a retry button, a 15-second client-side timeout (the original request had none, so a slow provider response and a hung network connection were indistinguishable from "still working"), and a degraded fallback: if summarization failed twice in a row, the UI showed the raw meeting notes with a banner explaining AI summarization was temporarily unavailable, so the feature's actual job, letting someone read their meeting notes, still worked even while the summarization step didn't.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

