{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Backoff, timeouts, and knowing when to stop retrying</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Always give a network call a timeout. Without one, a request that never comes back looks identical to one that's "still working," and the UI has no way to know it should give up and show an error. A simple client-side timeout (<code>AbortSignal.timeout(15000)</code> or similar) turns "hung forever" into "failed after 15 seconds," which is a state the UI can actually react to.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      For a rate limit specifically, respect the <code>Retry-After</code> header when the API sends one rather than guessing a backoff interval, and cap the number of automatic retries, two or three attempts with exponential backoff is a reasonable default before falling through to the degraded state, an unbounded retry loop against a provider that's actively rate-limiting you is part of the same problem, not a fix for it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Log the failure rate of the AI call path specifically, not just generic request errors, a spike in <code>429</code>/<code>5xx</code> responses from one upstream is exactly the signal that should page someone before the support queue does, and let the team make a proactive call (a status banner, a temporary "AI summaries are running slowly" notice) instead of finding out from a wave of tickets. At larger scale, a circuit breaker that stops sending new requests to a provider that's already failing consistently (rather than letting every user's request individually time out) reduces both wasted load on a struggling provider and the number of users who have to personally wait through a timeout before seeing the fallback.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

